      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208916/assets/0fc4d1e69587fe42eeae88a5382638fa9cf6c7c1f83c6c82a607401ab0de4cec.png)\n\n \n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(-1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nThe function $f^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208916/assets/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(4)) & ={f}^{-1}(-1) \\\\ 4 & ={f}^{-1}(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$",
